  {:name "Skin"
   :tissue-type :skin
   :model {:type :hyperelastic
           ;; 1.0e5 -> 1.18e6. THE OLD VALUE IS BELOW EVERYTHING READ.
           ;; The entry claimed a "low-strain effective E in [50,150] kPa" and
           ;; cited nobody. Three quantities were read for human skin and all
           ;; three are ABOVE 0.1 MPa:
           ;;   0.60 MPa   3 * the GOH ground-substance shear modulus
           ;;              mu = 0.2014 MPa (Ni Annaidh 2012b)          ->  6x
           ;;   1.18 MPa   the measured INITIAL SLOPE  (Ni Annaidh 2012a) -> 12x
           ;;   83.3 MPa   the measured LINEAR-REGION modulus (2012a)  -> 833x
           ;; The scalar takes the initial slope, because that is the same
           ;; quantity the old note claimed to hold -- the low-strain stiffness
           ;; -- and because it is a directly measured number rather than a
           ;; constitutive-fit parameter. Even its -1 SD bound, 0.30 MPa, is
           ;; three times the value that was here.
           :youngs-modulus 1.18e6
           ;; NOT SOURCED. No source read here measured a Poisson's ratio or a
           ;; density for skin.
           :poissons-ratio 0.45
           :density 1100
           :ultimate-tensile-strength 2.16e7
           :failure-strain 0.54
           :strain-regimes
           {:basis "Ni Annaidh 2012a, 56 excised human back-skin samples in uniaxial tension at 0.012 per second. ONE SPECIMEN SET, TWO MODULI, 70x APART: skin's stress-strain curve is a toe followed by a stiff collagen-dominated region, and the specimens did not fail until 54% strain. Which of these two numbers is right depends entirely on how far the caller is stretching the tissue."
            :initial {:youngs-modulus 1.18e6 :sd 8.8e5
                      :note "The initial slope. SD is 75% of the mean."}
            :linear-region {:youngs-modulus 8.33e7 :sd 3.49e7
                            :note "The paper's \"mean elastic modulus\". Reached only at large strain, well past anything a soft-body contact model runs at."}}
           :goh-model
           {:basis "Ni Annaidh 2012b, Gasser-Ogden-Holzapfel fit to human dermis, parameters by least-squares curve fitting of experimental test data. Carried for reference; the scalar above does NOT come from this fit."
            :ground-substance-shear-modulus 2.014e5
            :k1 243.6
            :k2 0.1327
            :fibre-dispersion 0.1404
            :fibre-dispersion-sd 0.0028}
           :directional
           {:basis "Langer lines. Ni Annaidh 2012a ESTABLISHES the anisotropy statistically and does not publish it as numbers: a MANOVA found the mechanical properties depend on Langer-line orientation at P<0.0001 to P=0.046, and 2012b found the Langer-line direction correlates with the preferred collagen fibre orientation in the dermis at p<0.001, R^2 = 0.95."
            :per-orientation-values :not-in-abstract
            :site-dependence "Location on the back also matters, and is likewise given as significance and not as values: UTS P=0.0002, elastic modulus P=0.001, strain energy P=0.0052."}}
   :source "Ni Annaidh A et al. 2012 J Mech Behav Biomed Mater 5(1):139-148 (PMID 22100088): initial slope 1.18 +/- 0.88 MPa, elastic modulus 83.3 +/- 34.9 MPa, UTS 21.6 +/- 8.4 MPa, failure strain 54% +/- 17%, n = 56. + Ni Annaidh A et al. 2012 Ann Biomed Eng 40(8):1666-1678 (PMID 22427196): GOH fit to human dermis, mu = 0.2014 MPa, k1 = 243.6, k2 = 0.1327, dispersion kappa = 0.1404 +/- 0.0028. SPECIMEN: excised human skin from the BACK, full thickness, cadaveric (MeSH: Humans, Skin, Dermis, Aged 80 and over). DIRECTION: uniaxial in-plane tension; the tissue is anisotropic about the Langer lines and the per-orientation moduli are NOT in either abstract, so this entry carries a direction-averaged number and says so. RATE: 0.012 per second -- slow, not impact. REGIME: the scalar is the INITIAL SLOPE. The same specimens give 83.3 MPa in the linear region, 70x stiffer; see :strain-regimes. CHANGED HERE: :youngs-modulus 1.0e5 -> 1.18e6, because 1.0e5 cited nobody and lies below every quantity read."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :sourced
    :sources
    [{:citation "Ni Annaidh A, Bruyere K, Destrade M, Gilchrist MD, Ottenio M. Characterization of the anisotropic mechanical properties of excised human skin. J Mech Behav Biomed Mater. 2012;5(1):139-148."
      :doi "10.1016/j.jmbbm.2011.08.016"
      :pmid "22100088"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"we have investigated the influence of location and orientation on the deformation characteristics of 56 samples of excised human skin. Uniaxial tensile tests were carried out at a strain rate of 0.012 s(-1) on skin from the back.\" And the numbers, verbatim: \"The mean ultimate tensile strength (UTS) was 21.6+/-8.4 MPa, the mean failure strain 54%+/-17%, the mean initial slope 1.18+/-0.88 MPa, the mean elastic modulus 83.3+/-34.9 MPa and the mean strain energy was 3.6+/-1.6 MJ/m(3).\" And the anisotropy, verbatim: \"A multivariate analysis of variance has shown that these mechanical properties of skin are dependent upon the orientation of the Langer lines (P<0.0001-P=0.046). The location of specimens on the back was also found to have a significant effect on the UTS (P=0.0002), the elastic modulus (P=0.001) and the strain energy (P=0.0052).\""
      :caveat "TWO MODULI FROM ONE SPECIMEN SET, 70x APART, AND THE ABSTRACT NAMES BOTH. Taking either one as \"the\" modulus of skin is a choice about strain, not about skin. The anisotropy is measured and REPORTED ONLY AS SIGNIFICANCE -- there is no per-Langer-line modulus in the abstract, so the scalar carried here is averaged over orientation and this entry cannot answer \"how stiff is skin along the Langer lines\". Excised cadaveric back skin under in-plane tension is also not the same loading as in-vivo indentation, which is where the tens-of-kPa figures in the older literature come from; the two are not in contradiction, they are different experiments, and this entry now says which one it holds."}
     {:citation "Ni Annaidh A, Bruyere K, Destrade M, Gilchrist MD, Maurini C, Ottenio M, Saccomandi G. Automated estimation of collagen fibre dispersion in the dermis and its contribution to the anisotropic behaviour of skin. Ann Biomed Eng. 2012;40(8):1666-1678."
      :doi "10.1007/s10439-012-0542-3"
      :pmid "22427196"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"The mean dispersion factor for the dermis was kappa = 0.1404+/-0.0028. The constitutive parameters mu, k(1) and k(2) were evaluated through physically-based, least squares curve-fitting of experimental test data. The values found for mu, k(1) and k(2) were 0.2014 MPa, 243.6 and 0.1327, respectively.\" And, verbatim: \"a definite correlation between the orientation of Langer lines and the preferred orientation of collagen fibres in the dermis (p < 0.001, R(2) = 0.95) was observed.\""
      :caveat "mu = 0.2014 MPa is the GOH ground-substance shear modulus, NOT a Young's modulus and NOT the initial slope. For an incompressible isotropic ground substance it corresponds to E = 3mu = 0.60 MPa, which is half the measured initial slope from the same group -- the difference is the collagen term k1/k2, which carries the rest. It is recorded here as a third independent number above 0.1 MPa, and the scalar does not come from it."}]}}

  {:name "Liver"
   :tissue-type :organ
   ;; :hyperelastic IS NOT FROM THE SOURCE. Rouvière's measurement is a
   ;; small-amplitude harmonic shear stiffness, i.e. a LINEAR quantity. The tag
   ;; is inherited from the uncited era and is left alone because liver really
   ;; is nonlinear at large deformation -- but no source read here fitted a
   ;; hyperelastic law to it, and the tag should not be read as evidence.
   :model {:type :hyperelastic
           ;; 3.0e3 -> 6.0e3, AND THE OLD VALUE LOOKS LIKE A UNIT-OF-QUANTITY
           ;; ERROR RATHER THAN A BAD GUESS. The old note read "liver E in
           ;; [1,5] kPa per MR elastography". MR elastography does not report a
           ;; Young's modulus. It reports a SHEAR stiffness, and for healthy
           ;; human liver that number is 2.0 +/- 0.3 kPa. Writing an MRE figure
           ;; into a Young's-modulus field understates E by a factor of three,
           ;; which is exactly the gap between 2-3 kPa and the value below.
           ;;
           ;; THE CONVERSION IS AN IDENTITY, AND IT IS WRITTEN OUT SO IT CAN BE
           ;; ARGUED WITH. E = 2G(1+nu); at the incompressible limit nu -> 1/2
           ;; that is E = 3G = 6.0 kPa. At this entry's own nu = 0.45 it would
           ;; be 5.8 kPa -- a 3% difference, five times smaller than the
           ;; source's own +/-0.3 kPa (15%) standard deviation, so the choice of
           ;; nu does not matter at the precision available. 6.0e3 is the
           ;; incompressible value because that is the convention every MRE
           ;; paper converts under.
           ;;
           ;; This is NOT the nucleus-pulposus situation. That tissue's shear
           ;; stress relaxes NEARLY TO ZERO, so it has no static modulus to
           ;; derive. Liver has a finite, repeatable shear stiffness; deriving E
           ;; from it is applying an identity, not inventing a solid.
           :youngs-modulus 6.0e3
           :shear-modulus 2.0e3
           :shear-modulus-sd 3.0e2
           ;; NOT SOURCED. Neither Poisson's ratio nor density was measured by
           ;; any source read here.
           :poissons-ratio 0.45
           :density 1060
           :capsule
           {:basis "Karimi & Shojaei 2018, 20 human cadavers, excised Glisson's capsule. A DIFFERENT TISSUE FROM THE PARENCHYMA ABOVE and the reason a single 'liver' modulus is a simplification worth naming: the capsule is the membrane the parenchyma is wrapped in, and it is roughly two orders of magnitude stiffer in compression."
            :tensile-youngs-modulus-axial 1.216e4
            :tensile-youngs-modulus-transverse 7.17e3
            :compressive-youngs-modulus-axial 1.9654e5
            :compressive-youngs-modulus-transverse 1.1241e5
            :note "Anisotropic by 1.7x in tension and 1.75x in compression, and roughly 16x stiffer in compression than in tension. NO NUMBER IN THE :model SCALARS ABOVE COMES FROM THIS."}}
   :source "Rouviere O et al. 2006 Radiology 240(2):440-448 (PMID 16864671): mean liver SHEAR stiffness 2.0 +/- 0.3 kPa in 12 healthy volunteers by MR elastography. The :youngs-modulus 6.0e3 is E = 3G under incompressibility, written out in the :model comments. SPECIMEN: 12 healthy human volunteers in vivo (eight men, four women, mean age 26.7 y, range 19-39), transcostal approach. DIRECTION: none -- MRE inversion returns an ISOTROPIC shear stiffness, and no source read here measured a direction dependence for liver parenchyma. RATE: NOT RECORDED. The abstract does not state the mechanical drive frequency, and MRE stiffness is frequency-dependent, so this entry cannot say at what rate 2.0 kPa applies. CHANGED HERE: :youngs-modulus 3.0e3 -> 6.0e3, because 3.0e3 cited nobody and appears to be an MRE shear stiffness written into a Young's-modulus field."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :sourced
    :sources
    [{:citation "Rouviere O, Yin M, Dresner MA, Rossman PJ, Burgart LJ, Fidler JL, Ehman RL. MR elastography of the liver: preliminary results. Radiology. 2006;240(2):440-448."
      :doi "10.1148/radiol.2402050606"
      :pmid "16864671"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"The mean liver shear stiffness was significantly lower in volunteers (mean, 2.0 kPa +/- 0.3 [standard deviation]) than it was in patients with liver fibrosis (mean, 5.6 kPa +/- 5.0; median, 3.7 kPa; range, 2.7-19.2 kPa; P < .001).\" Method, verbatim: \"liver stiffness was measured with MR elastography in 12 healthy volunteers (eight men, four women; mean age, 26.7 years; age range, 19-39 years) by using the subcostal approach and the transcostal approach\", and \"In volunteers, the transcostal approach surprisingly yielded better shear waves in the liver than did the subcostal approach.\""
      :caveat "THE DRIVE FREQUENCY IS NOT IN THE ABSTRACT, and MRE shear stiffness rises with frequency, so 2.0 kPa is a number without the rate that would make it reproducible. That is a real gap and is recorded rather than filled with the 60 Hz that most hepatic MRE happens to use. Note also what the same sentence says about the fibrosis arm: 2.7-19.2 kPa. A single healthy-liver modulus describes one end of a range that disease moves by an order of magnitude, so this scalar is a healthy-volunteer value and nothing else."}
     {:citation "Karimi A, Shojaei A. An Experimental Study to Measure the Mechanical Properties of the Human Liver. Dig Dis. 2018;36(2):150-155."
      :doi "10.1159/000481344"
      :pmid "29131053"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :supplies-number? false
      :read "Verbatim: \"The results revealed the tensile elastic modulus of 12.16 +/- 1.20 (mean +/- SD) and 7.17 +/- 0.85 kPa under the axial and transversal loadings respectively. Correspondingly, the compressive elastic modulus of 196.54 +/- 13.15 and 112.41 +/- 8.98 kPa were observed under the axial and transversal loadings respectively.\" And: \"The capsule showed a stiffer behavior under the compressive load compared to the tensile one. In addition, the axial elastic modulus of the capsule was found to be higher than that of the transversal one.\" Specimen, verbatim: \"20 human cadavers were autopsied and their liver capsules were excised\"."
      :caveat "THIS PAPER MEASURES THE CAPSULE, NOT THE PARENCHYMA, AND NO SCALAR IN THIS ENTRY COMES FROM IT -- hence :supplies-number? false. It is recorded because it is the strongest available evidence that \"liver\" is not one material: the capsule is anisotropic in both tension and compression, about 16x stiffer in compression than tension, and its compressive modulus is roughly 30x the parenchyma's Young's modulus carried above. A whole-organ model with one isotropic modulus has averaged that structure away."}]}}

  {:name "Tendon"
   :tissue-type :tendon
   :model {:type :linear-elastic
           ;; 1.2 GPa IS THE SOURCE'S OWN NUMBER, not a value that happens to
           ;; sit inside a band. Maganaris & Paul 1999 report the Young's
           ;; modulus of human tibialis anterior tendon in vivo as 1.2 GPa at
           ;; maximum isometric load. The value carried here predates the
           ;; citation and is unchanged.
           :youngs-modulus 1.2e9
           ;; NOT SOURCED. No source read here measured a Poisson's ratio for
           ;; tendon; 0.40 is inherited from the uncited era and is retained
           ;; only because removing it would hand the fea bridge its 0.3
           ;; default silently.
           :poissons-ratio 0.40
           ;; NOT SOURCED. Same status as the Poisson's ratio.
           :density 1100
           :directional
           {:basis "Maganaris & Paul 1999, human tibialis anterior tendon in vivo, five males, neutral ankle. The tendon was loaded along its long axis by percutaneous stimulation of the muscle; there is one direction in this experiment."
            :not-measured "Transverse and shear moduli. A tendon loaded across its fibres is a different and far lower number, and no source read here measured it."}}
   :source "Maganaris CN & Paul JP 1999 J Physiol 521(Pt 1):307-313 (PMID 10562354): Young's modulus 1.2 GPa. SPECIMEN: human tibialis anterior tendon, IN VIVO, five males, neutral ankle position; tendon force from joint moment / MRI moment arm, elongation from real-time ultrasonography. DIRECTION: along the tendon long axis, in tension. RATE: quasi-static ramped isometric contraction driven by increasing percutaneous stimulation voltage, not impact. REGIME: this is the modulus AT MAXIMUM ISOMETRIC LOAD (stress 25 MPa, strain 2.5%), and the paper's own conclusion is that even there the tendon is still inside the nonlinear 'toe' region -- so 1.2 GPa is a secant modulus at one physiological load, not the linear-region modulus of a tendon stretched to failure."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :sourced
    :sources
    [{:citation "Maganaris CN, Paul JP. In vivo human tendon mechanical properties. J Physiol. 1999;521(Pt 1):307-313."
      :doi "10.1111/j.1469-7793.1999.00307.x"
      :pmid "10562354"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"The tendon force and displacement at maximum isometric load were 530 N and 4.1 mm, and the corresponding stress and strain values were 25 MPa and 2.5 %, respectively. The tendon stiffness and Young's modulus at maximum isometric load were 161 N mm-1 and 1.2 GPa, respectively.\" And the regime sentence, verbatim: \"These results are in agreement with previous reports on in vitro testing of isolated tendons and suggest that under physiological loading the TA tendon operates within the elastic 'toe' region.\" Method, verbatim: \"Measurements were taken in five males at the neutral ankle position\" and involved isometric dynamometry with percutaneous electrical stimulation, real-time ultrasonography for tendon origin displacement and cross-sectional area, and MRI for tendon length and moment arm."
      :caveat "A tendon still inside its toe region has a modulus that RISES with load, so 1.2 GPa is a point on a rising curve and not a ceiling. n = 5, one tendon, one joint angle, one species, and the modulus is a secant value at the single load the stimulator could reach -- it is not the linear-region modulus of a tendon pulled to failure, and it must not be read as one."}]}}

  {:name "Cartilage"
   :tissue-type :cartilage
   ;; :biphasic, NOT :hyperelastic. The previous tag asserted that this tissue
   ;; is nonlinearly stiffening, and no source said so. Keenan 2009 fits a
   ;; LINEAR biphasic model -- solid matrix plus interstitial fluid, three
   ;; coefficients -- and those three coefficients are what this entry now
   ;; carries. Trading an unsourced constitutive claim for the one the source
   ;; actually used is the point.
   :model {:type :biphasic
           ;; THE OLD ENTRY WROTE AN AGGREGATE MODULUS INTO :youngs-modulus,
           ;; AND ITS OWN :source SAID SO: "articular cartilage compressive
           ;; aggregate modulus approx 0.5-1.5 MPa". That is the category error
           ;; kotoba.biomech.tissue/aggregate-modulus exists to name -- H_A is
           ;; the equilibrium stiffness of a laterally CONFINED specimen and is
           ;; a different experiment from a Young's modulus.
           ;;
           ;; THE CONFLATION SURVIVED BECAUSE OF A COINCIDENCE, AND THE
           ;; COINCIDENCE IS ITSELF SOURCED. For an isotropic linear elastic
           ;; solid, H_A = E(1-nu) / ((1+nu)(1-2nu)). At Keenan's measured
           ;; nu = 0.05 the factor is 0.95 / (1.05 * 0.90) = 1.0053, so H_A and
           ;; E differ by 0.53% -- roughly 100x smaller than the 0.48-1.58 MPa
           ;; spread Keenan measured across five sites of ONE tibial plateau.
           ;; Writing 8.0e5 in both fields is therefore defensible to the
           ;; precision the source supports. They remain different quantities,
           ;; and they now have different field names.
           :youngs-modulus 8.0e5
           :aggregate-modulus 8.0e5
           :aggregate-modulus-range [4.8e5 1.58e6]
           ;; 0.40 -> 0.05. THE LARGEST CORRECTION IN THIS FILE, AND THE OLD
           ;; VALUE CITED NOBODY. Keenan 2009 measured Poisson's ratio
           ;; 0.00-0.05 in human cartilage; 0.05 is the HIGH end of that band
           ;; and is taken here for the same reason the nucleus takes the low
           ;; end of its |G*| band -- a named endpoint of a measured range is
           ;; readable, a midpoint of it is invented.
           ;;
           ;; DIRECTION OF THE ERROR, STATED. Isotropic bulk modulus is
           ;; K = E / (3(1-2nu)). At nu = 0.40 that is 1.667 E; at nu = 0.05 it
           ;; is 0.370 E. The old value made cartilage 4.5x more resistant to
           ;; volume change than the only measurement read here says it is. A
           ;; near-zero Poisson's ratio is not an oddity of this dataset: it is
           ;; what a biphasic fit reports, because at equilibrium the fluid has
           ;; left and the drained solid matrix barely resists volume change.
           :poissons-ratio 0.05
           :poissons-ratio-range [0.0 0.05]
           ;; Keenan's third coefficient. The LOW end of 1.7-5.4 x 10^-15
           ;; m^4/(N*s), named rather than averaged, on the same convention as
           ;; the modulus above.
           :permeability 1.7e-15
           :permeability-range [1.7e-15 5.4e-15]
           ;; NOT SOURCED. No source read here weighed cartilage.
           :density 1100
           :sites
           {:basis "Keenan 2009: creep indentation at five sites across ONE human tibial plateau. The 0.48-1.58 MPa spread is BETWEEN THOSE FIVE SITES, not between donors -- a threefold range inside a single joint surface."
            :per-site-values :not-in-abstract
            :independent-agreement "Athanasiou 1991 measured the same three coefficients by the same method across five species and found site to matter in a stated direction: the anterior patellar groove has the LOWEST aggregate modulus, the HIGHEST permeability and the LOWEST Poisson's ratio of all sites tested. Its abstract publishes no number, so no value here comes from it -- but it is an independent statement that one scalar cannot describe this tissue across a joint."}}
   :source "Keenan KE et al. 2009 Comput Methods Biomech Biomed Engin 12(4):415-422 (PMID 19675978): human cartilage aggregate modulus 0.48-1.58 MPa, Poisson's ratio 0.00-0.05, permeability 1.7-5.4 x 10^-15 m^4/(N*s). SPECIMEN: five sites across a human tibial plateau, in vitro (species and joint confirmed from the PubMed MeSH record -- Humans, Knee Joint, Tibia, In Vitro Techniques -- not from the abstract text). LOADING: creep indentation, fitted with a linear biphasic finite-element interpolant; the modulus is therefore an EQUILIBRIUM confined stiffness, not a transient or impact response. RATE: quasi-static creep to equilibrium. CHANGED HERE: :poissons-ratio 0.40 -> 0.05, because 0.40 cited nobody and 0.00-0.05 is what was measured; and the aggregate modulus now has its own field instead of masquerading as a Young's modulus."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :unsourced-but-bounded
    :sources
    [{:citation "Keenan KE, Kourtis LC, Besier TF, Lindsey DP, Gold GE, Delp SL, Beaupre GS. New resource for the computation of cartilage biphasic material properties with the interpolant response surface method. Comput Methods Biomech Biomed Engin. 2009;12(4):415-422."
      :doi "10.1080/10255840802654319"
      :pmid "19675978"
      :pmcid "PMC2858459"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :read "Verbatim: \"Creep indentation tests were performed on five sites across a tibial plateau.\" And: \"These sites provided a representative range of aggregate moduli (0.48-1.58 MPa), Poisson's ratio (0.00-0.05) and permeability (1.7 x 10(- 15)-5.4 x 10(- 15) m(4)/N s) values found in human cartilage.\" Method, verbatim: \"Three biphasic material properties (aggregate modulus, Poisson's ratio and permeability) can be determined using an analytical or finite element model combined with optimisation to find the material properties values that best reproduce an experimental creep curve.\""
      :caveat "THE 8.0e5 CARRIED HERE IS NOBODY'S MEASUREMENT. It sits inside 0.48-1.58 MPa and is unchanged from the uncited era, exactly as the ligament's 5.0e8 sits inside Neumann's 423-1095 MPa. The abstract gives a RANGE ACROSS FIVE SITES OF ONE PLATEAU and no mean, so there is no single number to adopt; picking a midpoint would invent one. A PMC identifier exists but the Europe PMC fullTextXML endpoint returns 404 for it, so the per-site table was not read."}]
    :unobtained
    [{:citation "Athanasiou KA, Rosenwasser MP, Buckwalter JA, Malinin TI, Mow VC. Interspecies comparisons of in situ intrinsic mechanical properties of distal femoral cartilage. J Orthop Res. 1991;9(3):330-340."
      :doi "10.1002/jor.1100090304"
      :pmid "2010837"
      :could-not-obtain :numbers-not-in-abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :note "The abstract was read in full and is entirely qualitative on the quantities this entry carries. It is corroborating and directional: \"The aggregate modulus of the anterior patellar groove within each species is the lowest among all sites tested, and the permeability of the patellar groove cartilage is the highest and does not vary among species. Similarly, the Poison's ratio in the patellar groove is the lowest in all species\", and \"For any given location, no differences were found in the aggregate modulus among all the species\". That last clause is the one worth keeping: it says a bovine or canine number is not automatically wrong for human cartilage AT THE SAME SITE -- and that the site is what matters. NO NUMBER IN THIS ENTRY COMES FROM ATHANASIOU."}]}}

  {:name "Ligament"
   :tissue-type :ligament
   :model {:type :linear-elastic
           :youngs-modulus 5.0e8
           :poissons-ratio 0.40
           :density 1100}
   :source "Neumann 1992 J Biomech 25(10):1185-1194 (PMID 1400518): ALL \"overall\" tensile modulus 759 MPa (S.D. 336), i.e. 423-1095 MPa. The 5.0e8 Pa carried here sits inside that band and is unchanged. DIRECTION: along the fibre axis, in tension; a ligament loaded across its fibres is a different and much lower number this entry does not carry. RATE: ~1.0% strain per second (2.5 mm/s crosshead); ligament is rate-dependent and this does not describe an impact."
   :provenance
   {:sources
    [{:citation "Neumann P, Keller TS, Ekstrom L, Perry L, Hansson TH, Spengler DM. Mechanical properties of the human lumbar anterior longitudinal ligament. J Biomech. 1992;25(10):1185-1194."
      :doi "10.1016/0021-9290(92)90074-b"
      :pmid "1400518"
      :obtained :abstract
      :read "Verbatim: \"The average tensile strength, the 'overall' tensile modulus and the 'overall' strain of the ALL at failure were 27.4 MPa (S.D. 5.9), 759 MPa (S.D. 336) and 4.95% (S.D. 1.51), respectively.\" Bone-ALL-bone specimens from young human cadaveric motion segments, distracted to failure at 2.5 mm/s (\"approximately 1.0% strain per second\")."
      :caveat "759 MPa is an \"overall\" modulus of a bone-ligament-bone complex, not a mid-substance material modulus, and the paper's own result is that the strain field is not uniform: \"Peak substance strains were over twofold greater than peak strains at the ligament insertion sites\" and across the width \"peak strains in the outer portion of the ligament were over 40% greater than in the central region\". A single modulus averages that away. This is the ANTERIOR LONGITUDINAL ligament specifically; it is used here as the representative spinal ligament because it is the one whose modulus could actually be read."}]
    :unobtained
    [{:citation "Pintar FA, Yoganandan N, Myers T, Elhagediab A, Sances A. Biomechanical properties of human lumbar spine ligaments. J Biomech. 1992;25(11):1351-1356."
