  {:name "Adipose-Tissue"
   :tissue-type :organ
   :model {:type :hyperelastic
           ;; 3.0e3 -> 1.6e3. THE OLD VALUE WAS SILENTLY VISCERAL FAT.
           ;; Alkhouli 2013 measured paired subcutaneous and omental samples
           ;; from the same 19 subjects and they differ significantly:
           ;;   subcutaneous  initial 1.6 +/- 0.8 kPa   final 11.7 +/- 6.4 kPa
           ;;   omental       initial 2.9 +/- 1.5 kPa   final 32 +/- 15.6 kPa
           ;; The carried 3.0 kPa is 1.9x the subcutaneous initial modulus and
           ;; is within 4% of the OMENTAL one. A generic "Adipose-Tissue" in a
           ;; body model is subcutaneous fat -- the layer under the skin that a
           ;; contact model deforms -- so the scalar is now the subcutaneous
           ;; initial modulus, and the omental values sit beside it under
           ;; :sites rather than being averaged in.
           :youngs-modulus 1.6e3
           ;; NOT SOURCED. No source read here measured a Poisson's ratio or a
           ;; density for adipose tissue.
           :poissons-ratio 0.45
           :density 950
           :sites
           {:basis "Alkhouli 2013: human adipose tissue from 44 surgical subjects, tensile force/extension, PAIRED subcutaneous and omental samples in 19 of them. Two depots, two strain regimes, four numbers, and the spread between them is 20x. The tissue is nonlinear up to 30% strain, so 'initial' and 'final' are the two ends of one curve and not two materials."
            :subcutaneous {:initial-youngs-modulus 1.6e3 :initial-sd 8.0e2
                           :final-youngs-modulus 1.17e4 :final-sd 6.4e3
                           :note "The depot under the skin. This is where the scalar comes from."}
            :omental {:initial-youngs-modulus 2.9e3 :initial-sd 1.5e3
                      :final-youngs-modulus 3.2e4 :final-sd 1.56e4
                      :note "Visceral fat, significantly stiffer at both ends (P = 0.001 initial, P < 0.001 final)."}}
           :directional
           {:basis "Sommer 2013 found human abdominal adipose tissue to be ANISOTROPIC under multiaxial loading, and attributes it to aligned collagenous septa seen histologically."
            :per-direction-values :not-in-abstract
            :note "Alkhouli's tensile moduli, which is where every number in this entry comes from, are uniaxial and carry no direction. So this entry knows the tissue is anisotropic and cannot say by how much."}}
   :source "Alkhouli N et al. 2013 Am J Physiol Endocrinol Metab 305(12):E1427-E1435 (PMID 24105412): human subcutaneous adipose initial modulus 1.6 +/- 0.8 kPa, final 11.7 +/- 6.4 kPa; omental 2.9 +/- 1.5 and 32 +/- 15.6 kPa. SPECIMEN: adipose tissue from 44 human subjects undergoing surgery, 19 of them contributing PAIRED subcutaneous and omental samples. SITE: the scalar is SUBCUTANEOUS. DIRECTION: none carried -- Alkhouli's tests are uniaxial, and Sommer 2013 establishes that the tissue is anisotropic without publishing the values, so the anisotropy is recorded as unmeasured here rather than assumed absent. RATE: quasi-static force/extension; the tissue is nonlinear up to 30% strain and its stress relaxation follows a two-exponential time course, so a single modulus describes one point of one curve. REGIME: the scalar is the INITIAL modulus; the final modulus at 30% strain is 7x larger. CHANGED HERE: :youngs-modulus 3.0e3 -> 1.6e3, because 3.0e3 cited nobody and is within 4% of the OMENTAL value rather than the subcutaneous one. RETRIEVAL: this abstract is NOT in Europe PMC's record for this PMID; it came from Crossref."
   :provenance
   {:scalar :sourced
    :sources
    [{:citation "Alkhouli N, Mansfield J, Green E, Bell J, Knight B, Liversedge N, Tham JC, Welbourn R, Shore AC, Kos K, Winlove CP. The mechanical properties of human adipose tissues and their relationships to the structure and composition of the extracellular matrix. Am J Physiol Endocrinol Metab. 2013;305(12):E1427-E1435."
      :doi "10.1152/ajpendo.00111.2013"
      :pmid "24105412"
      :obtained :abstract
      :retrieved-via :crossref
      :read "Verbatim: \"AT was obtained from 44 subjects undergoing surgery. Force/extension and stress/relaxation data were obtained.\" And: \"AT showed nonlinear stress/strain characteristics of up to a 30% strain. Comparing paired subcutaneous and omental samples ( n = 19), the moduli were lower in subcutaneous: initial 1.6 +/- 0.8 (means +/- SD) and 2.9 +/- 1.5 kPa ( P = 0.001), final 11.7 +/- 6.4 and 32 +/- 15.6 kPa ( P < 0.001), respectively.\" And: \"The energy dissipation density was lower in subcutaneous AT ( n = 13): 0.1 +/- 0.1 and 0.3 +/- 0.2 kPa, respectively ( P = 0.006). Stress/relaxation followed a two-exponential time course.\""
      :caveat "EUROPE PMC RETURNS THIS RECORD WITH NO abstractText AT ALL, and a search that only used Europe PMC would have concluded the numbers were unobtainable. They came from Crossref, whose deposited abstract is the publisher's own. That is a retrieval difference, not a content difference, and it is recorded because the next person to look will hit the same empty field. Separately: these are TENSILE moduli. Subcutaneous fat under a body model is usually in compression, and no source read here measured adipose in compression."}]
    :unobtained
    [{:citation "Sommer G, Eder M, Kovacs L, Pathak H, Bonitz L, Mueller C, Regitnig P, Holzapfel GA. Multiaxial mechanical properties and constitutive modeling of human adipose tissue: a basis for preoperative simulations in plastic and reconstructive surgery. Acta Biomater. 2013;9(11):9036-9048."
      :doi "10.1016/j.actbio.2013.06.011"
      :pmid "23811521"
      :could-not-obtain :numbers-not-in-abstract
      :retrieved-via :europe-pmc-rest
      :note "This is the source that would supply the anisotropy. Its abstract was read in full and publishes NO constitutive parameter -- it states only that \"human abdominal adipose tissues under quasi-static and dynamic multiaxial loadings can be characterized as a nonlinear, anisotropic and viscoelastic soft biological material\" and that \"The aligned collagenous septa observed in histological investigations causes the anisotropy of the tissue\". The paper says its parameters \"are intended to serve as a basis for soft tissue simulations using the finite element method\" and then does not print them in the abstract. So this entry knows adipose tissue is anisotropic, on this paper's authority, and carries an isotropic scalar because the numbers that would replace it were not obtained. NO NUMBER IN THIS ENTRY COMES FROM SOMMER."}]}}
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; INTERVERTEBRAL DISC - two materials, not one.
  ;;
  ;; Added because a consumer already reasons about this tissue and this repo did
  ;; not carry it. cloud-itonami/suji divides a spinal compressive force by a disc
  ;; area to report :stress-mpa and cross-checks it against Wilke 1999's in-vivo
  ;; intradiscal pressure. It therefore has a STRESS and no modulus - it cannot
  ;; say how far the disc deforms. It also inherits Nachemson's pressure index,
  ;; whose entire content is that the pressure in the NUCLEUS is not the pressure
  ;; on the DISC. That is a material distinction, so it is two entries here and
  ;; not one averaged "disc".
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  {:name "Annulus-Fibrosus"
   :tissue-type :disc
   :sub-type :annulus-fibrosus
   :model {:type :anisotropic-linear-elastic
           ;; THE SCALAR IS THE AXIAL TENSILE MODULUS, and it is the least certain
           ;; of the three measured directions: 0.8 +/- 0.9 MPa has a standard
           ;; deviation LARGER THAN ITS MEAN. It is here because it is the axis a
           ;; spinal consumer loads, not because it is well determined.
           :youngs-modulus 8.0e5
           ;; nu21 outer, 0.67 +/- 0.22. THIS EXCEEDS 0.5 AND IS NOT AN ERROR: the
           ;; 1/2 bound follows from ISOTROPY and this tissue is not isotropic.
           ;; Fed to an isotropic solver it gives K = E/(3(1-2nu)) with
           ;; 1-2nu = -0.34, i.e. a NEGATIVE BULK MODULUS. See
           ;; kotoba.biomech.tissue/isotropically-admissible?, which exists because
           ;; kotoba.biomech.fem passes this field straight into fea's isotropic
           ;; linear-elastic material.
           :poissons-ratio 0.67
           ;; Confined-compression reference aggregate modulus H_A0, normal tissue.
           ;; A DIFFERENT QUANTITY FROM YOUNG'S MODULUS - the equilibrium stiffness
           ;; of a laterally confined specimen - so it gets its own field rather
           ;; than being written into :youngs-modulus, which would be a category
           ;; error no reader could see.
           :aggregate-modulus 5.6e5
           ;; THE OTHER FOUR NUMBERS FROM THE SAME SENTENCE OF THE SAME PAPER.
           ;; The entry used to carry H_A0 alone, which is the half of Iatridis
           ;; 1998 that says where the tissue ENDS UP and none of the half that
           ;; says how long it takes or how the law bends on the way. Reading
           ;; H_A0 without them is what produced 82% strain from sitting still.
           ;; sigma(offset), 0.13 +/- 0.06 MPa normal: the stress the tissue
           ;; already carries at zero strain. Subtracting it is not a fudge, it
           ;; is the constitutive law: sigma = sigma_offset + H_A0 * eps.
           :swelling-stress 1.3e5
           ;; k0, 0.20 +/- 0.10 x 10^-15 m^4/N-s. With H_A0 this fixes the
           ;; poroelastic diffusivity H_A0 * k0 and hence the time constant.
           :permeability 2.0e-16
           ;; beta, 2.13 +/- 1.48 normal. CARRIED BUT NOT USED by
           ;; kotoba.biomech.disc, which is linear; the disc namespace names it
           ;; in its :missing vector rather than pretending it does not exist.
           :nonlinear-stiffening-coefficient 2.13
           ;; M, 1.18 +/- 1.30, all specimens. Same status: measured, unused,
           ;; named. Its SD is larger than its mean.
           :permeability-strain-coefficient 1.18
           :directional
           {:basis "Elliott & Setton 2001, human AF, tension, linear region. 1 = circumferential, 2 = axial, 3 = radial."
            :circumferential-outer {:youngs-modulus 1.74e7 :sd 1.43e7}
            :circumferential-inner {:youngs-modulus 5.6e6 :sd 4.7e6}
            :axial {:youngs-modulus 8.0e5 :sd 9.0e5
                    :note "SD exceeds the mean; not significantly inhomogeneous outer vs inner."}
            :poissons-ratio {:nu21-outer {:value 0.67 :sd 0.22}
                             :nu21-inner {:value 1.6 :sd 0.7}
                             :nu12 {:value 1.8 :sd 1.4}
                             :nu13 {:value 0.6 :sd 0.7}}
            :tension-anisotropy-ratio 21.75
            :compression-anisotropy "NONE DETECTED. Iatridis 1998 tested axial and radial specimens in confined compression and found significant effects of degeneration BUT NOT ORIENTATION. So this tissue is about 22x anisotropic in tension and not measurably anisotropic in confined compression. One number is defensible for one of those and not the other."}}
   :source "Elliott & Setton 2001 J Biomech Eng 123(3):256-263 (PMID 11476369, tension, anisotropy) + Iatridis 1998 J Biomech 31(6):535-544 (PMID 9755038, confined compression). DIRECTION: the scalar :youngs-modulus is the AXIAL tensile linear-region modulus, 0.8 +/- 0.9 MPa. The circumferential outer modulus is 17.4 +/- 14.3 MPa, about 22x larger, and is in :directional. RATE: both studies are slow-rate quasi-static equilibrium protocols, not impact."
   :provenance
   {:sources
    [{:citation "Elliott DM, Setton LA. Anisotropic and inhomogeneous tensile behavior of the human anulus fibrosus: experimental measurement and material model predictions. J Biomech Eng. 2001;123(3):256-263."
      :doi "10.1115/1.1374202"
      :pmid "11476369"
      :obtained :abstract
      :read "Verbatim: \"There was evidence of significant inhomogeneity in the linear-region circumferential tensile modulus (17.4+/-14.3 MPa versus 5.6+/-4.7 MPa, outer versus inner sites) and the Poisson's ratio v21 (0.67+/-0.22 versus 1.6+/-0.7, outer versus inner), but not in the axial modulus (0.8+/-0.9 MPa) or the Poisson's ratios V12 (1.8+/-1.4) or v13 (0.6+/-0.7).\""
      :caveat "Poisson's ratios of 1.8 and 1.6 are admissible ONLY because the tissue is anisotropic; the 1/2 upper bound is a consequence of isotropy. A consumer that treats this entry as isotropic is wrong in a direction that produces a NEGATIVE BULK MODULUS rather than a visible failure."}
     {:citation "Iatridis JC, Setton LA, Foster RJ, Rawlins BA, Weidenbaum M, Mow VC. Degeneration affects the anisotropic and nonlinear behaviors of human anulus fibrosus in compression. J Biomech. 1998;31(6):535-544."
      :doi "10.1016/s0021-9290(98)00046-3"
      :pmid "9755038"
      :obtained :abstract
      :read "Confined compression of axial and radial specimens from the anterior outer region of L2-3. Verbatim: \"Significant effects of degeneration but not orientation were detected for the reference stress offset, sigma(offset), and parameters describing the compressive stiffness (i.e. reference aggregate modulus, H(A0), and nonlinear stiffening coefficient, beta). Average values were 0.13+/-0.06 and 0.05+/-0.05 MPa for sigma(offset), 0.56+/-0.21 and 1.10+/-0.53 MPa for H(A0) and 2.13+/-1.48 and 0.44+/-0.61 for beta for all normal and degenerate specimens, respectively.\" The 0.56 MPa taken here is the NORMAL value.\" And the permeability sentence, verbatim: \"No significant effect of degeneration or orientation were detected for either of the parameters describing the strain-dependent permeability (i.e. reference permeability, k0 and strain-dependent permeability coefficient, M) with average values for all specimens of 0.20+/-0.10 x 10(-15) m4/N-s and 1.18+/-1.30 for k0 and M, respectively.\" And the conclusion this entry rests on, verbatim: \"The significant effects of degeneration reported in this study suggested a shift in load carriage from fluid pressurization and swelling pressure to deformation of the solid matrix of the AF.\""
      :caveat "H_A0 is a REFERENCE modulus at the zero-strain state of a NONLINEAR model: the same sentence reports a stiffening coefficient beta = 2.13 +/- 1.48 for normal tissue. Treating the tissue as linear therefore OVERSTATES deformation at finite strain. kotoba.biomech.disc says so and refuses rather than returning the overstated number."}]
    :unobtained
    [{:citation "Ebara S, Iatridis JC, Setton LA, Foster RJ, Mow VC, Weidenbaum M. Tensile properties of nondegenerate human lumbar anulus fibrosus. Spine. 1996;21(4):452-461."
      :doi "10.1097/00007632-199602150-00009"
      :pmid "8658249"
      :could-not-obtain :numbers-not-in-abstract
      :note "The abstract WAS read in full and is corroborating but NON-NUMERIC. It states the directions only: \"The anterior anulus fibrosus had larger values for tensile moduli and failure stresses than the posterolateral anulus. Also, the outer regions of the anulus had greater moduli and failure stresses and lower failure strains than the inner regions.\" That is the same outer > inner ordering Elliott measures numerically, from an independent specimen set, so it is recorded as agreement. NO NUMBER IN THIS ENTRY COMES FROM EBARA."}]}}

  {:name "Nucleus-Pulposus"
   :tissue-type :disc
   :sub-type :nucleus-pulposus
   :model {:type :biphasic
           ;; NO :youngs-modulus, DELIBERATELY. Iatridis 1997 measured this tissue's
           ;; shear stress relaxing NEARLY TO ZERO - it is fluid-like in a transient
           ;; test. A Young's modulus is a property of an elastic solid. The
           ;; isotropic identity E = 2G(1+nu) would produce a number from the shear
           ;; modulus below, and that number would be an invention applied to a
           ;; tissue the source says is not a solid. The accessor returns nil, which
           ;; is a supported answer.
           :youngs-modulus nil
           ;; Likewise absent: a static Poisson's ratio for a tissue that relaxes to
           ;; zero shear stress has no meaning. Absent is also SAFE - see
           ;; isotropically-admissible?, which cannot pass a tissue it cannot check.
           :poissons-ratio nil
           ;; Dynamic shear: the LOW END of the measured |G*| band. Not a midpoint -
           ;; averaging 7 and 20 would invent a value at an unstated frequency.
           :shear-modulus 7.0e3
           :shear-modulus-range [7.0e3 2.0e4]
           :loss-angle-deg-range [23.0 30.0]
           :angular-frequency-range-rad-s [1.0 100.0]
           ;; Confined compression, nondegenerate.
           :aggregate-modulus 1.01e6
           :swelling-stress 1.38e5
           ;; ka, 0.9 +/- 0.43 x 10^-15 m^4/N-s nondegenerate. FOUR AND A HALF
           ;; TIMES the anulus k0, which is why the two tissues have time
           ;; constants an order of magnitude apart for the same disc height.
           :permeability 9.0e-16
           ;; NO :nonlinear-stiffening-coefficient AND NO
           ;; :permeability-strain-coefficient, DELIBERATELY. Johannessen &
           ;; Elliott state their method verbatim: \"Linear biphasic theory was
           ;; used to determine the permeability of the tissue.\" Nobody in the
           ;; sources read here fitted a nonlinear law to human nucleus, so
           ;; there is no beta to carry. That absence is a different fact from
           ;; the anulus's beta = 2.13 sitting unused, and the accessors return
           ;; nil for both only because nil is the honest answer to \"what did
           ;; the literature measure\", not because the two cases are alike.
           }
   :source "Iatridis 1997 J Biomech 30(10):1005-1013 (PMID 9391867): |G*| 7-20 kPa, loss angle 23-30 deg, over 1-100 rad/s. RATE IS THE WHOLE POINT: this tissue is fluid-like in relaxation and solid-like only dynamically, so a shear modulus without a frequency is meaningless. + Johannessen & Elliott 2005 Spine 30(24):E724-E729 (PMID 16371889): effective aggregate modulus 1.01 +/- 0.43 MPa nondegenerate, swelling stress 0.138 +/- 0.029 MPa. NO Young's modulus and NO Poisson's ratio are carried, deliberately: see the :model comments."
   :provenance
   {:sources
    [{:citation "Iatridis JC, Setton LA, Weidenbaum M, Mow VC. The viscoelastic behavior of the non-degenerate human lumbar nucleus pulposus in shear. J Biomech. 1997;30(10):1005-1013."
      :doi "10.1016/s0021-9290(97)00069-9"
      :pmid "9391867"
      :obtained :abstract
      :read "Verbatim: \"During stress-relaxation tests, the shear stress of the nucleus pulposus relaxed nearly to zero indicative of the fluid nature of the tissue. Under dynamic conditions, however, the nucleus pulposus exhibited predominantly 'solid-like' behavior with values for dynamic modulus (magnitude of G*) ranging from 7 to 20 kPa and loss angle (delta) ranging from 23 to 30 degrees over the range of angular frequencies tested (1-100 rad s-1).\""
      :caveat "THE ABSTRACT DOES NOT SAY WHICH ENDPOINT BELONGS TO WHICH FREQUENCY. It gives a band over a band. This entry therefore carries the range and takes the LOW end as its scalar; it does NOT claim 7 kPa is the 1 rad/s value, because that assignment was not read."}
     {:citation "Johannessen W, Elliott DM. Effects of degeneration on the biphasic material properties of human nucleus pulposus in confined compression. Spine. 2005;30(24):E724-E729."
      :doi "10.1097/01.brs.0000192236.92867.15"
      :pmid "16371889"
      :obtained :abstract
      :read "Verbatim: \"Degeneration produced significant decreases in swelling stress (Psw = 0.138 +/- 0.029 MPa nondegenerate, Psw = 0.037 +/- 0.038 MPa degenerate) and effective aggregate modulus (H(A)(eff) = 1.01 +/- 0.43 MPa nondegenerate, H(A)(eff) = 0.44 +/- 0.19 MPa degenerate).\" And the permeability sentence, verbatim: \"Permeability increased with degeneration (ka = 0.9 +/- 0.43 x 10(-15) m4/N-s nondegenerate, ka = 1.4 +/- 0.58 x 10(-15) m4/N-s degenerate).\" And the method, verbatim: \"Linear biphasic theory was used to determine the permeability of the tissue.\" And the conclusion, verbatim: \"Swelling is the primary load-bearing mechanism in both nondegenerate and degenerate nucleus pulposus.\""
      :caveat "That conclusion is why a linear elastic reading of this tissue is wrong IN KIND and not merely in magnitude: the in-vivo load path is osmotic swelling and fluid pressurisation, not deformation of a solid matrix."}]}}
]}
